981: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} gives the record-interface rigidity
982: and fixed-point refresh criterion that turns these finite projections into a
983: typed interface specification rather than a count of unpacking lemmas;
984: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} constructs finite publication
985: certificates under record-gate soundness; and
986: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} specializes the calculus
987: to the current six-row package.  The later results about packaging, upload
988: conditions, digests, command runs, inventories, venue records, and case snapshots
989: are deliberately theoremized audit-record consequences of finite rows and tables.
990: They document the support surface and its negative boundary; they should not be
991: read as independent mathematical invariants or as substitutes for the printed
992: TeX proofs in the core chain.
993: 
994: \subsection{Abstract finite-record interface}
995: 
996: For this long technical supplement and its compact CICM presentation artifact,
997: the following finite interface is the self-contained formal block used by every
998: later safety theorem.  It fixes, before use, the certificate tuple
999: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the dispatch table \(\Gamma\), the finite
1000: verifier predicate \(V\), the acceptance relation \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\), the
1001: record-gate-soundness predicate \(\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)\), and the
1002: stronger-reading preorder \(\preceq_{\Gamma}\).  All hypotheses in the headline
1003: safety theorem are finite-record hypotheses over these objects; none is supplied
1004: by a hidden semantic oracle, by the manual theorem inventory, or by generated
1005: prose.
1006: 
1007: \begin{center}
1008: \small
1009: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.27\linewidth}X}
1010: \toprule
1011: Term & Reviewer-facing meaning \\
1012: \midrule
1013: Record-gate soundness & Each accepted claim has the gate rows, evidence pointers,
1014: evidence level, and promotion boundary assigned by \(\Gamma\); this is a finite
1015: record predicate, not a proof that gate programs are correct \\
1016: Finite certificate & The tuple \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) read by the
1017: schema verifier for one claim: kind, level, evidence surface, human-promotion
1018: field, gate rows, discovery rows, and command boundary \\
1019: Finite bad subrecord & An inclusion-minimal subset of certificate fields whose
1020: absence or mismatch causes the first schema rejection; every negative result below
1021: points to such a missing coordinate \\
1022: Stronger reading & A claim \(c^{+}\) with all coordinates of \(c\) plus an added
1023: axis, evidence level, implementation, semantic, venue, support, theorem-ledger, or
1024: command-run coordinate \\
1025: Support coordinate & A finite upload, archive-equivalence, digest-binding,
1026: source-state, command-log, or inventory field that must be present before local
1027: support can be read externally \\
1028: No-free-upgrade & A stronger reading is accepted exactly when its added finite
1029: coordinate is supplied and passes the assigned gate; prose, inventory shape, or
1030: local digest rows alone do not upgrade the claim \\
1031: \bottomrule
1032: \end{tabularx}
1033: \end{center}
1034: 
1035: \begin{definition}[Operational acceptance interface]
1036: \label{def:operational-acceptance-interface}
1037: A \emph{claim} is a manuscript assertion that can affect a mathematical,
1038: artifact, route, or submission decision.  A \emph{load-bearing claim} is a claim
1039: whose removal would change a theorem statement, a case-study conclusion, or the
1040: submission-readiness status.  An \emph{advisory output} is any AI-agent draft,
1041: score, suggested theorem, review packet, or repair proposal before a gate records
1042: it.  A \emph{deterministic gate} is a repeatable predicate, script, or checklist
1043: whose inputs and pass/fail decision are recorded independently of the prose
1044: generated by an agent.  A \emph{human promotion} is the explicit editorial act
1045: that moves a candidate into publication state, for example by creating an active
1046: \texttt{2026\_*} paper directory with \texttt{main.tex} and \texttt{PIPELINE.md}.
1047: An \emph{evidence surface} is the set of source paths, commits, commands, logs,
1048: inventories, and witness records cited for a claim.  An \emph{acceptance entry}
1049: is a manuscript or submission-ledger record that marks a candidate claim as
1050: usable in the publication-facing argument.  \emph{Accepted work} is the subset of
1051: candidate claims with such an entry.  This is a status notion only: the
1052: definition records that an editorial acceptance entry exists, but it does not by
1053: itself assert external editorial acceptance or implementation correctness.  A
1054: pipeline is \emph{record-gate-sound for acceptance} when each acceptance entry records
1055: the claim kind, the evidence surface, the evidence level, and a pass record for
1056: every gate in $\Gamma$ assigned to that claim kind, and when a route-boundary
1057: entry also records human promotion.
1058: 
1059: The deterministic dispatch function $\Gamma$ from claim kinds to gates is part
1060: of the operational interface.  Its domain is
1061: \[
1062: \{\text{formal-source},\text{ traceability},\text{ route},
1063: \text{ submission},\text{ artifact},\text{ discovery},\text{ work-selection}\}.
1064: \]
1065: It sends formal-source claims to the build and axiom-purity gates; traceability
1066: claims to inventory and marker-existence gates; route claims to promotion,
1067: overlap, and submission-state gates; submission claims to submission-pack gates;
1068: artifact claims to status and limitation-ledger gates; discovery claims to the
1069: pinned discovery gate above and, for extension claims, to the theorem-content
1070: delta gate; and work-selection claims to the scheduler gate, whose evidence
1071: surface is a critical-path score or backlog window together with the claim kind
1072: that the selected item is meant to advance.  For mixed claims, $\Gamma$ returns
1073: the union of the gate sets for the claim kinds present.
1074: 
1075: The route gates have separate operational meanings.  The \emph{promotion gate}
1076: uses the active-paper evidence surface, namely a human promotion act producing a
1077: \path{2026_*} directory with \path{main.tex} and \path{PIPELINE.md}; passing
1078: means the candidate may be daemon-visible publication work, and failing means it
1079: remains intake or source material.  The \emph{overlap gate} uses route identity,
1080: semantic-overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths, and program-board state;
1081: passing means advancement is route-disjoint or has a recorded resolution, and
1082: failing means advancement is blocked until closure, merge, supersession, or
1083: waiting for prior feedback.  The \emph{submission-state gate} uses submission
1084: markers, venue state, board state, and submission-pack metadata; passing means
1085: the route's publication state permits the proposed action, and failing means the
1086: action would duplicate, race, or under-specify an active or submitted route.  The
1087: \emph{theorem-content delta gate} uses theorem inventories, hard-lint output, and
1088: delta records such as \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}; passing means that an
1089: extension claim identifies load-bearing mathematical content, and failing means
1090: that compilation, file churn, or surface edits may not be reported as substantive
1091: theorem growth.
1092: 
1093: A \emph{reviewable artifact} for this paper is either a public repository file,
1094: a supplemental file included with the submission, or an explicit
1095: historical-evidence boundary stating that the local path is used only as
1096: path-verified evidence and not as a fresh command-run claim.  A \emph{case
1097: witness record} is path-complete when its evidence surface gives a
1098: concrete source or artifact path for each load-bearing component of the row.  A
1099: route-overlap witness has the fields \emph{route identity}, \emph{submitted
1100: sibling}, \emph{overlap reason}, \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed next
1101: actions}.  An artifact-limitation witness has the fields \emph{artifact status
1102: path}, \emph{limitation ledger path}, \emph{observed limitation}, \emph{gate
1103: decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript claim}.  A promotion witness has the
1104: fields \emph{candidate surface}, \emph{human promotion evidence},
1105: \emph{active-paper markers}, \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript
1106: claim}.  A theorem-content witness has the fields \emph{changed surface},
1107: \emph{theorem inventory or hard-lint surface}, \emph{substantive-content delta},
1108: \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript claim}.  The inventory files
1109: \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}, \path{SOURCE_MAP.md}, and
1110: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} are part of the evidence surface
1111: for this paper and give the compact path catalogue used by
1112: Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}.
1113: 
1114: We distinguish two evidence levels.  A claim is \emph{path-verified} when this
1115: paper records the relevant source path, artifact path, pinned commit or local
1116: publication path, and gate decision, but does not assert that a command was
1117: freshly rerun for this submission.  A claim is \emph{command-run} when the paper
1118: also records the command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path
1119: for a fresh run.  This long technical supplement uses path-verified architecture
1120: and case evidence unless explicitly marked command-run.
1121: 
1122: A uniform \emph{pass record} is the finite tuple
1123: \[
1124: \rho=(g,I,d,e,\ell),
1125: \]
1126: where $g$ is the gate name, $I$ is the input surface submitted to that gate,
1127: $d$ is the recorded decision, $e$ is the evidence pointer supporting the
1128: decision, and $\ell$ is the evidence level.  A pass record is assigned to a
1129: claim certificate only when its evidence pointer is contained in the claim's
1130: evidence surface, in a submitted manifest or table row, in a bundled snapshot, or
1131: in an explicit historical-evidence boundary.
1132: 
1133: A \emph{publication certificate} for a load-bearing claim $c$ is the finite
1134: record
1135: \[
1136: \mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B).
1137: \]
1138: Here $K$ is the nonempty finite set of claim kinds carried by $c$, $\ell$ is the
1139: evidence level, $E$ is the evidence surface, $H$ is either a human-promotion
1140: record or the marker that $c$ does not cross the intake-to-publication boundary,
1141: $R$ gives one pass record, with the five fields above, for each gate in
1142: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $D$ is present exactly when $K$ contains discovery
1143: and then names the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used for the
1144: claim, and $B$ records either the command-run fields or the explicit boundary
1145: that no command-run assertion is made.  A certificate is \emph{locally
1146: checkable} when these finite fields are present and every path, manifest row, or
1147: log named by a field lies in the submitted artifact, the supplemental review
1148: bundle, or the explicit path-verified historical boundary.
1149: 
1150: For later use we make the local check explicit as a finite verifier.  The
1151: concrete package checker supplied with the review bundle is
1152: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  Its positive invocation is
1153: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}; it reads exactly
1154: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
1155: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
1156: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
1157: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and
1158: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and writes the recorded pass log
1159: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  Its negative-example
1160: invocation is
1161: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json},
1162: with recorded log \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md};
1163: that fixture upgrades a route row to command-run strength while omitting the
1164: command-run tuple and is rejected with the finite bad subrecord
1165: \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  The mathematical verifier below is
1166: the schema abstraction of this package checker.
1167: 
1168: Let \(\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)\) be the seven-kind domain and let
1169: \(\mathsf{Lev}=\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}\).  For a candidate record
1170: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), define six finite predicates:
1171: \begin{enumerate}
1172: \item[$P_1$:] $K\ne\emptyset$, $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, and
1173: $\ell\in\mathsf{Lev}$.
1174: \item[$P_2$:] $E$ is finite and each item of $E$ is a submitted path,
1175: review-bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit
1176: historical-evidence boundary.
1177: \item[$P_3$:] for every gate $g\in\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $R$ contains a
1178: pass record $(g,I,d,e,\ell')$ whose evidence pointer lies in $E$ or in a named
1179: submitted table, manifest row, bundled snapshot, or historical boundary.
1180: \item[$P_4$:] $H$ records the human promotion exactly when $c$ crosses the
1181: intake-to-publication boundary and otherwise records that no such boundary is
1182: crossed.
1183: \item[$P_5$:] $D$ is present exactly when discovery lies in $K$, and then names
1184: the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used by the claim.
1185: \item[$P_6$:] $B$ either records command, source commit, environment, exit code,
1186: and log path for command-run evidence or explicitly records that no command-run
1187: assertion is made.
1188: \end{enumerate}
1189: Define the finite verifier
1190: \[
1191: V(c,\mathcal C)=\bigwedge_{i=1}^{6}P_i(c,\mathcal C,\Gamma).
1192: \]
1193: If this conjunction is false, the rejected subrecord is the finite set of fields
1194: read by the least-indexed failed predicate.  This paragraph defines the verifier
1195: only; no unprinted semantic oracle is part of it.  The next definition packages
1196: the same finite fields as an explicit input type and a finite acceptance table;
1197: the exactness theorem below then proves that the verifier and the independently
1198: named record-acceptance relation coincide on that finite data structure.
1199: 
1200: For a package $P$ with ledger $L$, dispatch table $\Gamma$, and submitted
1201: support surface $S$, write
1202: \[
1203: \mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)
1204: \]
1205: for the following finite predicate.  For every acceptance entry of a
1206: load-bearing claim $c$ in $L$, there is a publication certificate
1207: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ such that $E\subseteq S$, $V(c,\mathcal C)=1$,
1208: each pass record in $R$ is a submitted row of $L$, the row decision is passing,
1209: and the row's evidence pointer denotes an element of $S$ at the evidence level
1210: recorded in the row.  If $B$ does not contain command, source state,
1211: environment, exit code, and log path, then $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$ asserts only this
1212: finite row-and-pointer property and asserts no implementation-level correctness
1213: of the gate that produced the row.
1214: \end{definition}
1215: 
1216: \begin{definition}[Record-gate soundness external interface]
1217: \label{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}
1218: The external record-gate interface for a package is the finite predicate
1219: \[
1220: \mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)
1221: \]
1222: with the following inputs and outputs.  Its inputs are exactly a package $P$, a
1223: submitted support surface $S$, a finite acceptance ledger $L$, and the deterministic
1224: claim-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$ of
1225: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  Its positive output is the
1226: finite family of certificates
1227: \[
1228: \{\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B): c\in L
1229: \text{ is an accepted load-bearing claim}\}
1230: \]
1231: such that $E\subseteq S$, $V(c,\mathcal C(c))=1$, every assigned gate in
1232: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$ has a submitted passing row in $R$, each evidence
1233: pointer in such a row denotes an element of $S$ at the recorded evidence level,
1234: the human-promotion coordinate $H$ is present exactly at an intake-to-publication
1235: crossing, and the discovery and command-boundary coordinates $D$ and $B$ have the
1236: presence conditions required by the verifier predicates $P_5$ and $P_6$.  Its
1237: negative output, when the predicate fails for a ledger row, is only the finite bad
1238: subrecord read by the first failed verifier predicate among $P_1,\ldots,P_6$.
1239: 
1240: This interface makes no further claim.  In particular it does not assert that an
1241: external venue accepted the work, that a command was freshly rerun, that a Lean
1242: development builds, that an artifact has the semantic property named in prose, or
1243: that an advisory agent output is proof evidence, unless the corresponding source
1244: path, command, source state, environment, exit code, log path, or gate row is one
1245: of the finite coordinates read above.
1246: \end{definition}
1247: 
1248: \begin{proposition}[Record-gate interface equivalence and non-claim boundary]
1249: \label{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}
1250: For the operational acceptance interface of
1251: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}, the standalone interface in
1252: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} is equivalent to the
1253: predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ used in the publication-safety theorem.
1254: Moreover, under this interface every consequence used by
1255: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} is determined by the finite
1256: coordinates $(P,S,L,\Gamma)$ and by the displayed certificates
1257: $\mathcal C(c)$; no implementation-level, semantic, venue-acceptance,
1258: fresh-execution, or advisory-evidence claim follows from record-gate soundness
1259: unless it appears as one of those finite coordinates.
1260: \end{proposition}
1261: 
1262: \begin{proof}
1263: The predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ in
1264: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} quantifies over exactly the
1265: accepted load-bearing ledger entries $c\in L$ and requires exactly the following
1266: data: a certificate $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$, containment $E\subseteq S$,
1267: acceptance by the finite verifier $V$, submitted passing rows for the pass records
1268: in $R$, and evidence pointers denoting elements of $S$ at the recorded evidence
1269: levels.  The verifier definition expands $V(c,\mathcal C(c))=1$ into the six
1270: predicates $P_1,\ldots,P_6$, which are precisely the kind, evidence-surface,
1271: assigned-gate, human-promotion, discovery, and command-boundary presence
1272: conditions listed in
1273: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}.  Hence a positive
1274: instance of one definition supplies a positive instance of the other, with the
1275: same certificates and the same submitted rows; conversely the standalone
1276: interface supplies every finite field required by the original predicate, so the
1277: original predicate holds.
1278: 
1279: For the boundary statement, inspect the fields read by $P_1,\ldots,P_6$.  They
1280: contain claim kinds, evidence levels, finite evidence pointers, human-promotion
1281: markers, assigned gate rows, discovery rows, and the command-run or no-command-run
1282: boundary.  None of these fields is a venue decision, a proof-assistant semantic
1283: judgment, an artifact semantic evaluator, a live daemon state, or an advisory
1284: agent proof object unless an additional row explicitly records that fact as an
1285: evidence coordinate satisfying the same verifier predicates.  Therefore the
1286: interface can be used in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} only to
1287: construct finite publication certificates and finite bad subrecords.  Any stronger
1288: reading would require an added coordinate and is outside the predicate just proved
1289: equivalent.
1290: \end{proof}
1291: 
1292: \begin{definition}[Stronger-reading preorder]
1293: \label{def:stronger-reading-preorder}
1294: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and certificate tuple
1295: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ of
1296: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  A proposed reading
1297: $c^{+}$ of the same prose claim is \emph{record-stronger} than $c$, written
1298: $c\preceq_{\Gamma} c^{+}$, if at least one of the following finite coordinates is
1299: strictly enlarged while the others are unchanged or enlarged: the claim-kind set
1300: $K$; the evidence level, from $\mathsf{path}$ to $\mathsf{command}$; the evidence
1301: surface $E$; the promotion field $H$; the assigned gate-pass family $R$; the
1302: discovery-interface field $D$; or the boundary field $B$.  It is
1303: \emph{properly stronger} if the enlargement is strict in at least one coordinate.
1304: It is \emph{accepted as a stronger reading} only when the enlarged tuple still
1305: satisfies $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$: every newly added kind has its assigned gates,
1306: every newly raised evidence level has the command tuple required by A6, and every
1307: new evidence item is on the submitted support surface or in an explicit
1308: historical boundary.
1309: 
1310: The preorder is intentionally syntactic and finite-record based.  It is not a
1311: semantic entailment relation between mathematical propositions, and it does not
1312: turn an inventory entry, digest row, or path reference into proof evidence for a
1313: theorem-like environment.
1314: \end{definition}
1315: 
1316: \begin{definition}[Certificate-schema acceptance table]
1317: \label{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}
1318: Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$ of
1319: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  The verifier input for a
1320: load-bearing claim $c$ is the following finite record type: